Wt and Jm are exact complements of each other and both returned 1. That already rules out every AND-type law entirely: for AND(a,b) to equal 1 at an all-1s input, both literals must be non-negated (true when prop=1); but then both are false at an all-0s input, forcing AND=0 there — contradicting Jm=1. So AND is eliminated regardless of which properties/signs.

For OR and XOR, satisfying both Wt=1 and Jm=1 requires the two literals to have *opposite* polarity (one positive, one negated) — same logic, worked either way. That leaves exactly 12 candidate hypotheses: 3 property-pairs × {OR,XOR} × 2 sign-assignments each.

I still have one free observation left. Checking all four untested symbols (Rc, Vx, Um, Bf) against the 12 surviving hypotheses, each splits them exactly 6/6 — all equally informative right now. I'll use Rc, since it also sets up a clean 3/3 split on the follow-up test (Bf) regardless of outcome.

